          "points": 1,
          "xp": 1,
          "accept": "There is a big discussion in the development community about using code comments vs. writing self-documenting code (e.g., see this Quora post - https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/questions/51307/self-documenting-code-vs-commented-code). Self-documenting code means that it is so clear that it does not need a code comment. Some important sources advocate against code comments and are in favor of self-documenting code:\n\n●​  *Code never lies; comments sometimes do. (Ron Jeffries)*\n●​  *A comment is a failure to express yourself in code. If you fail, then write a comment; but try not to fail. (Uncle Bob)*\n●​  *The comments will inevitably become out of date, and untrustworthy commentsare worse than no comments at all. (The Pragmatic Programmer)*\n●​  *A comment is the code’s way of asking to be clearer. (Kent Beck)*\n●​  *When you feel the need to write a comment, first try to refactor the code so that any comment becomes superfluous. (Martin Fowler)*\n\nIf you want to read more about self-documenting code, see this webpage: https://medium.com/codex/towards-self-documenting-code-371364bdccbb \nWhy are these people in favor of self-documenting code?",
